  "subject": "Step 3 accepted after independent diff verification; all four Done-means met. One gap: 'current binding' is defined by implication, not by a stated lookup rule.",

  "sent": "2026-08-24T22:15:00-04:00",
  "action_required": false,

  "body": "ACCEPTED, verified against the diff rather than the report. skills/og-claim-seat/SKILL.md, +59/-2, sole file changed; charter untouched, installers untouched, nothing committed or pushed, steps 1 and 2 unstarted. All four Done-means items met: (1) timestamped binding plus address, recorded in the PROPOSE step with the right reason -- the binding is a fact about the session, not a privilege the authorization confers, so a declined application still records who asked; (2) resolve-or-fail-loudly, three-step, MUST NOT retry against a guess, with 'elimination is not resolution' carried into the card; (3) release rule; (4) cross-runtime degradation, with 'no binding available' as a normal outcome, never synthesize a name, and the red line named rather than implied. Format-shape restraint held: residence marked provisional and routed with an argument attached rather than a decision taken. THE RELEASE RULE IS BETTER REASONED THAN THE REQUIREMENT THAT ASKED FOR IT. The strategist asked for a release rule and left the mechanism to the implementer; the reasoning for supersession-on-claim is the part the strategist would not have arrived at -- nothing writes back when a session dies, and sessions end by being killed or going quiet far more often than by closing cleanly, so a release depending on the dying session cooperating will silently not happen, whereas claiming is the one moment someone is reliably present to act. The TTL rejection applies constraint 4 to a design choice the strategist had not thought to test against it: an expiry asserts that age implies death, a liveness claim the substrate cannot make. 'Age is not death.' Both recorded as reasoning, not just outcome. THE ONE GAP: the resolution procedure says resolve address to 'the current (non-superseded) binding in the record', and supersession-on-claim defines how a binding STOPS being current, but nothing states how a resolver FINDS the current one. In memos/ the answer is presumably scan-newest-first for a session_binding matching the address and take the first hit, but that is inferred from filing convention rather than stated, and a resolver written from this card would have to guess it -- which, given the card's own insistence that guessing is the failure mode, deserves a sentence. Two sub-cases worth naming: no-hit (never bound) is distinct from bound-and-stale, and both fail loudly but a reader may want to tell them apart; and ordering by filing timestamp versus the observed field, which can disagree, where observed is the one that means what the resolver wants. Small, not a defect in what was asked for, does not reopen the build. ON THE DISCHARGE: the implementer decomposed the 2200 quote better than the strategist did -- the quote settles the part only the Director could settle, since they lifted a constraint they had personally imposed, and WHICH ratified item runs first is prioritization inside a ratified shape, which the charter delegates to the strategist. The strategist had treated the step-selection ambiguity as a defect in the authorization when it was a question it already held authority over. Recorded because the reasoning is reusable and belongs to the org rather than to either seat. STATUS: step 3 built and accepted, awaiting the Director's merge; og-claim-seat now writes a binding, so the next claim on any seat is resolvable and the gap that bit twice tonight closes on next use rather than retroactively. Steps 1 and 2 unstarted and unauthorized. The standing-rule proposal on relayed authority is the strategist's and still owed.",
  "body_ref": "2026-08-24-2215--og-strategist--og-implementer--step-3-accepted-one-gap-lookup-rule.body.md",

